# {{ project_name }}

STM32 firmware built with CMake. Board: blackpill (STM32F401CCU6), family STM32F4.

## Build (cross-compile, target hardware)

```sh
cmake --preset blackpill_f401
cmake --build --preset blackpill_f401
```

## Test (host, native compiler — not cross-compiled)

`test/` is a separate, self-contained CMake project (own `CMakeLists.txt`), not a
subdirectory of the firmware build. Configure and build it directly:

```sh
cmake -S test -B build/host-test
cmake --build build/host-test
ctest --test-dir build/host-test
```

## Layout

- `boards/<name>/` — board-specific MCU define, linker script, and startup file selection.
  Only `blackpill_f401` exists today. See `boards/README.md` before adding another.
- `src/app/` — board-agnostic application logic. This is what gets unit tested.
- `src/drivers/` — thin wrappers over the vendor HAL; the seam CMock mocks for host
  tests. Don't call HAL functions directly from `src/app/` — go through a driver wrapper
  so the app layer stays testable on the host.
- `third_party/` — pinned git submodules (CMSIS core, CMSIS device F4, F4 HAL driver,
  Unity, CMock). Don't edit these in place, and don't bump a pinned commit without
  checking the release notes for API changes across CMSIS-Core/CMSIS-Device/HAL — they
  must stay version-consistent.
- `build/host-test/mocks/` — CMock output, regenerated at build time from
  `src/drivers/*.h`. Never hand-edit; it lives in the build dir and is not committed.
- `tools/bootstrap.sh` — first-time submodule setup, and `--write-pins` to re-record
  pins after bumping a submodule.
- `docs/` — currently empty; add design notes here as real decisions accumulate.

## Gotchas

- Run `./tools/bootstrap.sh` before the first configure. Plain
  `git submodule update --init` does **not** work in a freshly generated project:
  Copier ships `.gitmodules` but no git history, so there are no gitlink entries and
  the command silently succeeds while cloning nothing. The script recreates the
  gitlinks from the `sha` pins in `.gitmodules` first.
- Two independent CMake configurations exist: the cross-build (`blackpill_f401` preset,
  `arm-none-eabi-gcc`, source dir = repo root) and the host test build (source dir =
  `test/`, system compiler). Never point one at the other's build directory.
- CMock requires `ruby` on PATH to generate mocks; it is not a CMake/C dependency.
- `-Wall -Wextra` come from the `project_warnings` interface target and apply only to
  our own code. Vendor includes are marked SYSTEM. Link `project_warnings` into any new
  target under `src/`; don't add warning flags globally.
